WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.841
Most supplier invoices are routine.
The risky one usually looks

00:00:05.876 --> 00:00:07.316
routine too.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.650
This one's from Hartree Systems.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.481
£14,820. Same supplier,

00:00:15.806 --> 00:00:20.764
same layout. But the bank
details here have changed.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.040
Invoice Operations Agent does
the matching for you.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.956
It checks every invoice against the
order and what was delivered,

00:00:31.281 --> 00:00:34.463
and gets the routine ones ready to pay.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.152
Anything it won't pay without a
person comes to one queue,

00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:43.691
with the reason and a recommendation.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.092
Here, the change came from a
lookalike email domain,

00:00:48.371 --> 00:00:53.131
three days before the invoice arrived.
So it recommends escalating,

00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:57.264
and nobody can approve the payment
until a callback is recorded,

00:00:57.705 --> 00:01:00.747
on the number you already hold.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.479
Escalate it, and the money stays
put, the owner is named,

00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:10.299
and the decision is on the record.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.903
It works the other side too. When
Alderwood promises to pay,

00:01:17.182 --> 00:01:23.567
the forecast updates, and the amount
at risk drops straight away.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.235
Your team stops re-checking the
routine, and puts its attention

00:01:29.444 --> 00:01:32.555
where the money is actually at risk.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.412
Invoice Operations Agent. Built by Innate.
